       "source": "packages/vscode/src/explorerHelp/manage-service.md",
-      "body": "# Service and runtime\n\nStopping the AIRK Service and reviewing its runtime are in AIRK Admin, Service. **Stop for restart** records a restart request and stops the service; the management page does not start a replacement. After it stops, run `airkctl service start` locally or use AIRK: Start AIRK Service in VS Code. Then run `airkctl admin open` for a new browser session or reopen management in the editor after verifying the service connection.\n\nAdvanced management uses AIRK Admin. Explorer also keeps Agents, Activity, Usage and evidence reads inside this window; choose the corresponding main destination for those native service results.\n\nChoose Open AIRK Admin to open the admin pages. Choose where... picks In VS Code (the Simple Browser, with a single-use sign-in link kept only in memory) or In my browser; the choice is remembered in the airk.admin.openIn setting. If the VS Code browser reports a certificate problem, choose Open in my browser.\n\n## Optional Arbiter stages\n\nThe approved source design separates **Arbiter Focus** (Qwen3-Reranker-0.6B) from **Arbiter Context — Compact** (Qwen3-1.7B) and **Arbiter Context — Large** (Qwen3-4B). Focus ranks MiniLM's retrieved candidates and can run with Context off. Context assesses evidence and guides bounded additional reads. Only one Context model is active. These controls require a service version with stage support and qualified profiles; inspect the running service rather than assuming this guide enables them.\n\nBoth stages are off by default and need their own explicit index enrollment and verified supported assets. Model selection is intended configuration; active readiness additionally needs task/platform support and current resources. Status, ordinary queries and Help never authorize model downloads. Use native Arbiter status and current generated command/schema guidance for supported controls.\n\nFocus still has a memory and latency cost; candidate count and passage length matter. There is no universal RAM floor or assumed GPU support. An unavailable stage retains ordinary retrieval with an explicit reason, while normal vector/completeness refusals still apply. Disablement drains native work; remove models through guarded service controls instead of deleting live assets. Native Help's Arbiter guide explains requirements, offline use, stage consent, fallback and recovery."
+      "body": "# Service and runtime\n\nStopping the AIRK Service and reviewing its runtime are in AIRK Admin, Service. **Stop for restart** records a restart request and stops the service; the management page does not start a replacement. After it stops, run `airkctl service start` locally or use AIRK: Start AIRK Service in VS Code. Then run `airkctl admin open` for a new browser session or reopen management in the editor after verifying the service connection.\n\nOpening **Service** reads current resources and Arbiter status automatically. **Refresh resources** and **Refresh Arbiter status** read them again. Resource summaries distinguish observed CPU and memory from configured reservations; unavailable measurements remain unavailable. Complete native results, including diagnostic fields, stay in **Details**. A retained Arbiter observation is labeled when a refresh fails.\n\nCertificate trust, start at sign-in and restart/stop are separate task sections. Advanced management uses AIRK Admin. Explorer also keeps Agents, Activity, Usage and evidence reads inside this window; choose the corresponding main destination for those native service results.\n\nChoose Open AIRK Admin to open the admin pages. Choose where... picks In VS Code (the Simple Browser, with a single-use sign-in link kept only in memory) or In my browser; the choice is remembered in the airk.admin.openIn setting. If the VS Code browser reports a certificate problem, choose Open in my browser.\n\n## Optional Arbiter stages\n\nThe approved source design separates **Arbiter Focus** (Qwen3-Reranker-0.6B) from **Arbiter Context — Compact** (Qwen3-1.7B) and **Arbiter Context — Large** (Qwen3-4B). Focus ranks MiniLM's retrieved candidates and can run with Context off. Context assesses evidence and guides bounded additional reads. Only one Context model is active. These controls require a service version with stage support and qualified profiles; inspect the running service rather than assuming this guide enables them.\n\nBoth stages are off by default and need their own explicit index enrollment and verified supported assets. Model selection is intended configuration; active readiness additionally needs task/platform support and current resources. Status, ordinary queries and Help never authorize model downloads. Choose a model and use its **Download** action to request assets explicitly. Downloading does not enable a stage or enroll indexes. Select the indexes and tasks that stage may read, choose **Enable**, then **Apply** that stage's choices. Turning one stage off keeps the other stage's choices. Use **Cancel** or **Recover** for a failed model operation and refresh status before another change whose outcome is unknown.\n\nFocus still has a memory and latency cost; candidate count and passage length matter. There is no universal RAM floor or assumed GPU support. An unavailable stage retains ordinary retrieval with an explicit reason, while normal vector/completeness refusals still apply. Disablement drains native work; remove models through guarded service controls instead of deleting live assets. Native Help's Arbiter guide explains requirements, offline use, stage consent, fallback and recovery.\n\nThe new 8192-token / 120-second default is an implementation target, not a claim that any optional profile passed its resource, latency or model-quality gates. Unqualified local-test profiles remain labeled as such; terminal decisions and final publication stay unavailable until their own evidence passes."
     },
     {
       "id": "management.storage",
@@ -1427,7 +1427,7 @@
         "agent-setup"
       ],
       "source": "crates/airk-mcp/src/help/content.json",
-      "body": "# Connecting a coding agent\n\nUse the discovered native HTTPS endpoint, never a guessed fixed port or retired --root SOURCE mcp launcher. airkctl agents connect writes only the endpoint URL and the response-mode header; no token, helper or connection is involved (U110). agents list reports older entries that still carry a token, a helper or a connection-id placeholder, and agents repair removes them. Native configuration writers preserve unrelated servers, comments, disabled states and policies; concurrent changes or unsafe ownership refuse and retain private displaced-file recovery where applicable.\nScope is a tool parameter. Applying a search scope in the Explorer stores it as the workspace default of every folder of that window (airkctl scope default --workspace FOLDER --index ID --set ID; no --index/--set clears; airkctl scope defaults lists them). Defaults persist across restarts and closed windows. An agent passes workspace (its working folder) or names index_ids/index_set_ids; clients that offer MCP roots fill workspace automatically.\nLoad changed entries in the actual external client and complete its trust controls. Claude Code may need its MCP reconnect action, Codex a restart and VS Code a server restart. A native report does not prove a running agent reconnected. Preserve complete refusal/recovery details and original operation identity."
+      "body": "# Connecting a coding agent\n\nUse the discovered native HTTPS endpoint, never a guessed fixed port or retired --root SOURCE mcp launcher. airkctl agents connect writes only the endpoint URL and the response-mode header; no token, helper or connection is involved (U110). agents list reports older entries that still carry a token, a helper or a connection-id placeholder, and agents repair removes them. Native configuration writers preserve unrelated servers, comments, disabled states and policies; concurrent changes or unsafe ownership refuse and retain private displaced-file recovery where applicable.\nIf automatic setup refuses, keep its complete policy/recovery details and use the canonical manual entry. Run airkctl agents manual --client codex --project [folder] --output json, selecting claude or vscode for those clients and --user for user scope. Replace the bracketed folder placeholder with the actual path. Native output names the exact file, format and complete entry text; merge it without replacing unrelated settings. Explorer and Admin Copy use that same native text. A manual entry grants no automatic file-edit authority.\nScope is a tool parameter. Applying a search scope in the Explorer stores it as the workspace default of every folder of that window (airkctl scope default --workspace FOLDER --index ID --set ID; no --index/--set clears; airkctl scope defaults lists them). Defaults persist across restarts and closed windows. An agent passes workspace (its working folder) or names index_ids/index_set_ids; clients that offer MCP roots fill workspace automatically.\nLoad changed entries in the actual external client and complete its trust controls. Claude Code may need its MCP reconnect action, Codex a restart and VS Code a server restart. A native report does not prove a running agent reconnected. Preserve complete refusal/recovery details and original operation identity."
     },
     {
       "id": "engine.request-recovery",
@@ -1608,7 +1608,7 @@
         "arbiter"
       ],
       "source": "crates/airk-mcp/src/help/content.json",
-      "body": "# Arbiter Focus, Context and model requirements\n\nMiniLM is the bundled candidate-retrieval encoder: it creates reusable document/question vectors for efficient indexed search. The approved two-stage source design adds independently enabled Arbiter Focus and Arbiter Context. Stage controls and profiles remain subject to actual native support and qualification; this Help text does not make a model available. Inspect the running catalog/status rather than assuming an older service implements these stages.\nArbiter Focus uses Qwen3-Reranker-0.6B to score a question and each retrieved passage together, improving the order of a bounded candidate set. It can run while Context is off. Focus cannot recover evidence MiniLM/native retrieval never selected. Relevance is not correctness, complete coverage or a calibrated sufficiency judgment.\nArbiter Context — Compact uses Qwen3-1.7B; Arbiter Context — Large uses Qwen3-4B. They perform the same bounded context-gathering task; size names do not promise a quality difference. Only one Context choice is active. With separately enabled and enrolled Focus, the flow is MiniLM candidates, Focus ranking, then Context evidence assessment and justified additional native reads. Focus is a shared ranking component, not a third Context choice.\nBoth optional stages are off by default and require explicit immutable-index enrollment for their own content processing. Enabling Focus does not enable Context or enroll all indexes; deletion/recreation/import does not inherit consent. Stage enrollment never expands an agent's read scope. Context uses Focus only where Focus is separately enabled and the index enrolled. For excluded indexes, no content contributes to that stage. Turning both stages off preserves ordinary native retrieval.\nThe answer read gathers cited contextual evidence for a question; context reads neighboring indexed lines at a coordinate. For a retry question, useful evidence may include a contract, implementation, test and document. Native gathering stays within original scope/publications and total read/round/fan-out/byte/token/deadline budgets, retains source receipts and gaps, and cannot write sources, run commands or become an autonomous agent loop. Agents and Workbench retain equivalent full canonical evidence, provenance and errors. Model assessments remain distinct from source facts; fewer follow-ups and better answers need independent measured evidence.\nUse airkctl arbiter status for actual native requirements, supported tasks, configuration revision, selected versus active profiles, availability and reasons. Downloaded weights, a visible choice or source implementation are not verified readiness or independent device/platform qualification. Focus is smaller than either Context model but still needs memory/CPU/backend admission; candidate count and passage length affect latency. There is no universal RAM minimum, assumed GPU support or silent model/precision/device switch. Account for Focus, the selected Context model, MiniLM and ordinary indexing together.\nRead command=\"arbiter\" and each exact subcommand for served inputs. airkctl arbiter configure --file [complete Configure request JSON file] uses the current Core-native generated schema; replace the bracketed placeholder with the actual path and never invent stage fields from prose. Provision/remove/cancel/recover are explicit native management operations bound to actual configuration/request identities. A status read or ordinary question never downloads or enrolls anything. Verify transfer/staging requirements, complete authenticated assets and supported execution before activation. Verified offline import has the same checks; once ready, inference stays local in the existing service with no cloud or sidecar.\nIf an optional stage is off, unsupported, missing assets or unavailable, retain ordinary retrieval and its explicit not-applied/refusal reason; do not claim the fallback performed optional ranking or assessment. Existing vector/hybrid completeness refusals still apply. Disablement suppresses new/stale optional delivery while actual native work and pins drain. Remove through the guarded native operation, not by deleting live files. Recover an uncertain original request instead of replaying it. Keep full canonical results available for authorized inspection, copy and export; disclose reordering, selected subsets, omissions and stop reasons."
+      "body": "# Arbiter Focus, Context and model requirements\n\nMiniLM is the bundled candidate-retrieval encoder: it creates reusable document/question vectors for efficient indexed search. The approved two-stage source design adds independently enabled Arbiter Focus and Arbiter Context. Stage controls and profiles remain subject to actual native support and qualification; this Help text does not make a model available. Inspect the running catalog/status rather than assuming an older service implements these stages.\nArbiter Focus uses Qwen3-Reranker-0.6B to score a question and each retrieved passage together, improving the order of a bounded candidate set. It can run while Context is off. Focus cannot recover evidence MiniLM/native retrieval never selected. Relevance is not correctness, complete coverage or a calibrated sufficiency judgment.\nArbiter Context — Compact uses Qwen3-1.7B; Arbiter Context — Large uses Qwen3-4B. They perform the same bounded context-gathering task; size names do not promise a quality difference. Only one Context choice is active. With separately enabled and enrolled Focus, the flow is MiniLM candidates, Focus ranking, then Context evidence assessment and justified additional native reads. Focus is a shared ranking component, not a third Context choice.\nBoth optional stages are off by default and require explicit immutable-index enrollment for their own content processing. Enabling Focus does not enable Context or enroll all indexes; deletion/recreation/import does not inherit consent. Stage enrollment never expands an agent's read scope. Context uses Focus only where Focus is separately enabled and the index enrolled. For excluded indexes, no content contributes to that stage. Turning both stages off preserves ordinary native retrieval.\nThe answer read gathers cited contextual evidence for a question; context reads neighboring indexed lines at a coordinate. For a retry question, useful evidence may include a contract, implementation, test and document. Native gathering stays within original scope/publications and total read/round/fan-out/byte/token/deadline budgets, retains source receipts and gaps, and cannot write sources, run commands or become an autonomous agent loop. Agents and Workbench retain equivalent full canonical evidence, provenance and errors. Model assessments remain distinct from source facts; fewer follow-ups and better answers need independent measured evidence.\nUse airkctl arbiter status for actual native requirements, supported tasks, configuration revision, selected versus active profiles, availability and reasons. Downloaded weights, a visible choice or source implementation are not verified readiness or independent device/platform qualification. Focus is smaller than either Context model but still needs memory/CPU/backend admission; candidate count and passage length affect latency. There is no universal RAM minimum, assumed GPU support or silent model/precision/device switch. Account for Focus, the selected Context model, MiniLM and ordinary indexing together.\nThe default request shares8192 input tokens and one120-second operation deadline across all stages, rounds and additional reads. Applicable clients allow5000ms for response transport, up to125seconds; that overhead grants no extra native work time. Focus retains its static512-token pair graph. Explicit historical2048-token/30-second controls remain historical. These limits are not measured latency or graph-fit guarantees; inspect actual resource and end-to-end receipts.\nThis1.8.x local candidate keeps unqualified models labeled. Current Context source policy and8192 compiler metadata require new native-generated assets; public availability remains pending their genuine immutable source publication. Terminal sufficiency, clarification and no-justified-read classes remain unavailable until separate suitable human-backed evidence passes. No quality gain, fitted terminal behavior or all-device support is inferred from available weights.\nRead command=\"arbiter\" and each exact subcommand for served inputs. airkctl arbiter configure --file [complete Configure request JSON file] uses the current Core-native generated schema; replace the bracketed placeholder with the actual path and never invent stage fields from prose. Provision/remove/cancel/recover are explicit native management operations bound to actual configuration/request identities. A status read or ordinary question never downloads or enrolls anything. Verify transfer/staging requirements, complete authenticated assets and supported execution before activation. Verified offline import has the same checks; once ready, inference stays local in the existing service with no cloud or sidecar.\nIf an optional stage is off, unsupported, missing assets or unavailable, retain ordinary retrieval and its explicit not-applied/refusal reason; do not claim the fallback performed optional ranking or assessment. Existing vector/hybrid completeness refusals still apply. Disablement suppresses new/stale optional delivery while actual native work and pins drain. Remove through the guarded native operation, not by deleting live files. Recover an uncertain original request instead of replaying it. Keep full canonical results available for authorized inspection, copy and export; disclose reordering, selected subsets, omissions and stop reasons."
     }
   ],
   "cliCommands": {
@@ -1616,7 +1616,7 @@
     "admin": "AIRK Admin in the browser: open it, or print a single-use sign-in URL\n\nUsage: airkctl admin [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>\n\nCommands:\n  open  Open AIRK Admin in this computer's default browser, signed in\n  url   Print a single-use AIRK Admin sign-in URL (valid 60 seconds) for manual use\n\nOptions:\n      --output <OUTPUT>\n          Result and error format: json writes one JSON document or error line\n          \n          [default: text]\n          [possible values: json, text]\n\n      --service-runtime-dir <SERVICE_RUNTIME_DIR>\n          The AIRK service's control directory\n\n      --service-project <SERVICE_PROJECT>\n          A registered project binding\n\n      --catalog-session <CATALOG_SESSION>\n          Explicit reviewed single-index session JSON; preferences alone grant no access\n\n      --combined-session <COMBINED_SESSION>\n          Frozen reviewed multi-Project session JSON; indexes are executed once each\n\n      --project-ids <PROJECT_IDS>\n          Narrow a combined read to explicit Projects from its reviewed session\n\n      --index-ids <INDEX_IDS>\n          Narrow a combined read to member indexes; requires explicit Project scope\n\n      --member-availability <MEMBER_AVAILABILITY>\n          Report member failures or require every selected member to answer\n          \n          [possible values: report, require]\n\n      --root <ROOT>\n          The project folder (default: the current folder)\n          \n          [default: .]\n\n      --data-dir <DATA_DIR>\n          The project's data folder (default: the installation's data location)\n\n      --collection <COLLECTION>\n          [default: default]\n\n  -h, --help\n          Print help\n",
     "admin open": "Open AIRK Admin in this computer's default browser, signed in.\n\nNeeds a local graphical session (refused over SSH). The single-use sign-in link goes through an owner-only file, never a command line.\n\nUsage: airkctl admin open [OPTIONS]\n\nOptions:\n      --output <OUTPUT>\n          Result and error format: json writes one JSON document or error line\n          \n          [default: text]\n          [possible values: json, text]\n\n      --runtime-dir <RUNTIME_DIR>\n          The service's control directory (default: --service-runtime-dir, else the installation's)\n\n      --page <PAGE>\n          The page to open\n          \n          [possible values: overview, index-library, activity, agents, storage, recovery, service, logs, usage, search, help]\n\n      --service-runtime-dir <SERVICE_RUNTIME_DIR>\n          The AIRK service's control directory\n\n      --for-folder <FOR_FOLDER>\n          Index library: prefill a new index for this folder\n\n      --service-project <SERVICE_PROJECT>\n          A registered project binding\n\n      --catalog-session <CATALOG_SESSION>\n          Explicit reviewed single-index session JSON; preferences alone grant no access\n\n      --show-index <SHOW_INDEX>\n          Index library: show this index (`idx_…`)\n\n      --combined-session <COMBINED_SESSION>\n          Frozen reviewed multi-Project session JSON; indexes are executed once each\n\n      --show-set <SHOW_SET>\n          Index library: show this index set (`prj_…`)\n\n      --project-ids <PROJECT_IDS>\n          Narrow a combined read to explicit Projects from its reviewed session\n\n      --index-ids <INDEX_IDS>\n          Narrow a combined read to member indexes; requires explicit Project scope\n\n      --member-availability <MEMBER_AVAILABILITY>\n          Report member failures or require every selected member to answer\n          \n          [possible values: report, require]\n\n      --root <ROOT>\n          The project folder (default: the current folder)\n          \n          [default: .]\n\n      --data-dir <DATA_DIR>\n          The project's data folder (default: the installation's data location)\n\n      --collection <COLLECTION>\n          [default: default]\n\n  -h, --help\n          Print help (see a summary with '-h')\n",
